course: dictionary
lesson: 15851
title: Unequal: not the same, not fair
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Unequal means not the same. Two things are unequal when one is bigger, larger in amount, or worth more than the other. «The two pizzas were unequal: one was big and the other was tiny.» The word gives a clue too: un- means not, and equal means the same.
explain: Unequal can also mean not fair. «The two teams had unequal chances, because one had to play on a muddy pitch.» People are treated unequally when they get different chances for no good reason. A good check is to swap in "not the same". If the sentence still makes sense, unequal is probably right.
explain: Unequal can be formal. «Ben was unequal to the task of climbing the steep hill» means he was not strong enough for the climb. This meaning is less common, so look at the words after unequal: if you see "to" and a job, think "not good enough for it".
fact: Unequal means not the same in size, amount or value.
fact: Unequal can also mean not fair, when people are treated differently or have different chances.
fact: Unequal to a task means not good enough or strong enough for it. This meaning is formal.
fact: In maths, unequal parts are not the same size, and fractions need equal parts.
fact: The opposite of unequal is equal, and the adverb is unequally.
exercise: «The two teams had unequal chances, as one played on a muddy pitch.» What does unequal mean here? | *Not fair, so people have different chances | Exactly the same | Very quick | Full of mud
exercise: In maths, what are unequal parts? | Parts that are the same size | Parts that are very small | *Parts that are not the same size | Parts with no numbers on them
exercise: Which word is the opposite of unequal? | Unhappy | *Equal | Unsafe | Uneven
test: What does «unequal treatment» of children mean? | Children given the same food | Children going to bed early | *Children treated differently in an unfair way | Children playing very fast
test: What does «Ben was unequal to the task of climbing the steep hill» mean? | Ben was equal in strength to the hill | Ben climbed the hill very quickly | Ben was happy about the climb | *Ben was not strong enough for the climb
exam: Which word fits the gap? «The two bags were ___ in weight, so the teacher shared the books again.» | *unequal | unfair | unequally | equal
